<p>The proliferation of automobiles and the expansion of their supporting infrastructure dealt an even more serious blow: after World War II, delivering mail on the roads had clearly become more efficient than doing it underground. Some&nbsp;“rocketeers,” as the system’s operators were called, stuck it out until&nbsp;December 12, 1953, when pneumatic mail service was officially suspended all across the country.</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1127993" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/13053053/Pneumatic-tube-mail.png" alt width="799" height="696" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/13053053/Pneumatic-tube-mail.png 799w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/13053053/Pneumatic-tube-mail-360x314.png 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/13053053/Pneumatic-tube-mail-240x209.png 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/13053053/Pneumatic-tube-mail-768x669.png 768w" sizes="(max-width: 799px) 100vw, 799px"></p>
<p>This marked the end of an era, especially for the rocketeers said to have used the network to&nbsp;surreptitiously&nbsp;receive orders from their favorite sandwich shops. Remembering Ted Stevens’ notorious remark, it’s tempting to describe the series of tubes that were pneumatic mail delivery systems as an early version of the internet — and even more so it was already getting people hooked on&nbsp;food delivery.</p>
